+# JIT and compile caches
+
+Large models on `vllm_base` and `sglang_base` spend minutes to tens of minutes compiling kernels at
+startup (TileLang, DeepGEMM, Triton, Inductor, FlashInfer, SGLang's own JIT kernels). sml keeps those
+caches on capstor, one directory per image and GPU architecture, so only the first launch of an image
+pays (#270).
+
+## What happens in a job
+
+1. **Key.** On the batch host, master.sh derives the image key.
+    - If the images CI wrote `<image>.sqsh.digest` (e.g. `sha256:...`), that is the key.
+    - Otherwise the key is `sqsh-<hash>` of the file size and the 96-byte squashfs superblock. That costs one
+      small read, and it changes on every rebuild even though images are updated in place.
+2. **GPU arch.** master.sh reads `compute_cap` from `nvidia-smi` (`sm90` on GH200).
+3. **Shared directory.** The job's cache directory is
+   `/capstor/store/cscs/swissai/infra01/sml/shared-caches/<key>/<arch>/`.
+4. **Seed.** Every node copies that directory into `/tmp/sml-jit-cache` before the engine starts. The env
+   file's `{jit_cache}` resolves to that path.
+5. **Promote.** Once the replica answers `/health`, and every 30 minutes after that, each node publishes
+   the entries it compiled back to the shared directory.
+
+The overlay path is fixed on purpose. Triton's group files and the ninja build trees of FlashInfer and
+tvm-ffi store absolute paths, so an entry is only reused at the path it was written at.
+
+## Why promotion is safe with concurrent jobs
+
+- New entries are copied into `<shared>/.staging/<host>-<pid>-<rand>/`, made group-readable, and then:
+  - a directory is `rename`d into place;
+  - a file is `link`ed into place.
+- Both operations fail if the target already exists. So the first writer wins, nothing is overwritten,
+  and a reader never sees a half-written entry.
+- No cross-node locks are needed. This matters because Lustre can be mounted `localflock` or `noflock`.
+- Files with an mtime younger than 60 s are deferred to the next round, in case a build is still writing
+  them in place.
+- Some files are never published:
+  - lock files (a stale `torch_extensions` lock blocks every later load forever);
+  - the libraries' temp files;
+  - node-specific state such as the GPU P2P check JSONs.
+
+## Modes
+
+```bash
+sml advanced ... --jit-cache overlay   # default
+sml advanced ... --jit-cache shared    # point the caches at capstor directly (no copy; needs flock on capstor)
+sml advanced ... --jit-cache off       # per-job caches only (today's behaviour)
+sml advanced ... --jit-cache-root DIR  # another root, e.g. on iopsstor
+```
+
+Users without write access to the root still seed and get warm starts; only promotion needs write
+access.
+
+## One-time setup (infra01 admin)
+
+```bash
+ROOT=/capstor/store/cscs/swissai/infra01/sml/shared-caches
+mkdir -p "$ROOT" && chgrp infra01 "$ROOT" && chmod 2775 "$ROOT"
+setfacl -d -m g::rwx "$ROOT"   # if ACLs are enabled on capstor
+```
+
+## Which variables
+
+The env TOMLs list them in `[env]`. Each points into `{jit_cache}`:
+
+- **vllm_base:** `VLLM_CACHE_ROOT`, `TRITON_CACHE_DIR`, `DG_JIT_CACHE_DIR`, `TILELANG_CACHE_DIR`,
+  `FLASHINFER_WORKSPACE_BASE`, `TVM_FFI_CACHE_DIR`, `CUTE_DSL_CACHE_DIR`, `CUDA_CACHE_PATH`.
+- **sglang_base:** `SGLANG_CACHE_DIR`, `SGLANG_JIT_CACHE_DIR` (it does not follow `SGLANG_CACHE_DIR`),
+  `TRITON_CACHE_DIR`, `TILELANG_CACHE_DIR`, `TVM_FFI_CACHE_DIR`, `CUTE_DSL_CACHE_DIR`.
+
+These lists were produced and checked with `cache-audit`. It runs a launch cold in a fresh HOME, records
+every file written, and fails CI when a new image writes a cache that no variable redirects. Rerun it
+when an image changes its engine version.
+
+vLLM keys its torch.compile cache by checkpoint path, so a new checkpoint recompiles its graphs. Its
+Triton, TileLang, DeepGEMM and FlashInfer kernels are shared across checkpoints of the same architecture.
+
+## Housekeeping
+
+```bash
+python3 src/swiss_ai_model_launch/assets/sml_jit_cache.py du --root "$ROOT"
+python3 src/swiss_ai_model_launch/assets/sml_jit_cache.py gc --root "$ROOT" --max-bytes 500e9 --max-age-days 30 --dry-run
+```
+
+`gc` evicts the oldest `<key>/<arch>` directories:
+- age is measured from `.meta/last_used`, which every seed touches;
+- it first removes directories unused for more than `--max-age-days`;
+- it then removes the least recently used ones until the total fits `--max-bytes`.
+
+An eviction is a rename into `.trash`, so a job that is seeding at that moment just starts cold. To drop
+a bad entry, delete its directory under `<key>/<arch>/<component>/`.
